const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What are the most important skills needed for a career change in India?',
    a: 'It depends on the target role, not a fixed list — but three categories matter every time: transferable skills you already have (communication, problem-solving, project management, stakeholder handling), the specific domain knowledge and tools your target field runs on, and visible proof that you can actually do the work, not just describe it. The World Economic Forum\'s Future of Jobs Report 2025 lists analytical thinking, resilience and adaptability, and technological literacy as the core skills employers rank highest right now, which is a reasonable starting checklist if you have no other signal yet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a certification to switch careers in India, or is a portfolio enough?',
    a: 'Neither alone is usually enough for a serious pivot, and neither is optional for most roles. Indian hiring has moved toward what recruiters describe as a hybrid model: a certification helps you pass the initial resume filter and shows you understand the field\'s vocabulary, but projects, case studies, and interview performance are what actually convert a shortlist into an offer. Certification alone tends to move pay by a small margin; certification paired with real project work moves it by a much larger one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it realistically take to close a skill gap before switching careers?',
    a: 'There is no single correct number — it depends on how far the gap is, how much time you can give it each week, and whether the skill needs practice or just knowledge. As a reference point, workplace reskilling research finds that focused, targeted upskilling programs typically bring someone to working competence in a few months, while some deep technical gaps (data science, software engineering, regulated fields) take considerably longer. Treat any fixed number you read, including this one, as a rough anchor, not a deadline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I focus on transferable skills or learn everything new for my target role?',
    a: 'Neither extreme works. Leading only with transferable skills gets you an interview but not an offer, because employers still need to see role-specific competence. Starting from zero and ignoring what you already have wastes years you do not need to spend. The efficient path is to audit what genuinely transfers, name it in the target role\'s language, and spend your limited learning time only on the real gaps that remain.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What skill gaps do most career changers underestimate?',
    a: 'Three come up repeatedly: domain knowledge (the unwritten context, jargon, and judgment calls insiders take for granted), specific tools and software the target role runs on daily (not the concept, the actual interface), and the gap between holding a certificate and being able to perform under real conditions with real stakeholders and real deadlines. All three are invisible until you are inside a live job description or a real interview, which is why the audit has to happen before you commit, not after.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I prove a new skill before I have a job that uses it?',
    a: 'Use a small, real piece of work instead of a claim. A portfolio project, a volunteer engagement, a paid freelance task, a contribution to an open dataset or open-source project, or a documented case study of a problem you solved on your own time all count as proof, as long as another person outside your head can see and evaluate the result. The proof does not need to be perfect or large — it needs to be real, checkable, and specific to the skill you are claiming.',
